    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4fbee7e9_cd6c74bb",
        "filename": "tests/net/jni/apf_jni.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 227,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-21T14:31:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Looks like this makes sure that the APF counters are captured in jdata. Why do you this only if you return false? It could be useful to know what the counters are even if all the packets are dropped.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 224,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 227,
        "endChar": 78
      },
      "revId": "f84ea617179f3d7a6a2d32815ba6bec671ffbadc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b6a8be2d_5ee5e017",
        "filename": "tests/net/jni/apf_jni.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 227,
      "author": {
        "id": 1385042
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-26T13:58:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think pass 0 to update data and free the space used by the copy is more proper than JNI_COMMIT since we don\u0027t need the copy after function return, right?\nUse memcpy() to copy counters from program_and_data to data_raw and then call ReleaseByteArrayElements() passing 0 as mode parameter so that we don\u0027t need SetByteArrayRegion() since counter will be copied back by ReleaseByteArrayElements().",
      "parentUuid": "989a07b9_ef156d46",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 227,
        "startChar": 67,
        "endLine": 227,
        "endChar": 76
      },
      "revId": "f84ea617179f3d7a6a2d32815ba6bec671ffbadc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b0381aab_9bcb4166",
        "filename": "tests/net/jni/apf_jni.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 227,
      "author": {
        "id": 1385042
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-26T13:58:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The counters will be only dumped when assert true failure. If dump counters when all the packets are dropped, we need to print the result to android log? or we can show the result after finishing the unit test?",
      "parentUuid": "4fbee7e9_cd6c74bb",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 224,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 227,
        "endChar": 78
      },
      "revId": "f84ea617179f3d7a6a2d32815ba6bec671ffbadc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6899130d_bb93ae12",
        "filename": "tests/net/jni/apf_jni.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 227,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-11-27T07:59:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Given that it\u0027s is easy to get the counters, we might as well get them. We could log them, or print them when running the test. But you\u0027re right, we could also just not dump them.\n\nThe only thing I\u0027d say is that if we only populate the counters on failure, then that\u0027s confusing, because the documentation of dropsAllPackets says \"populates data[] with the APF counters\". If we only dump them on failure then it should say that it only populates the counters on failure.",
      "parentUuid": "b0381aab_9bcb4166",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 224,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 227,
        "endChar": 78
      },
